## Part 3a - Open questions and recommendations: US-138026 (import)

Eleven questions are open for the import story. Two of them block the build: the sample file (Q16) and the file format (Q2). For each question the recommendation is the default the build will follow unless the team decides otherwise.

**Q2. Is the file fixed-width or pipe-delimited?** Reference: In Scope "Text File pipe delimited" versus AC1 "imported using the layout TPTI ADABAS File Offload.csv", which gives byte positions 1 to 250.

- Recommendation: build for fixed-width positions exactly as the layout states; confirm with the mainframe team before the package is finalised.
- Why: the layout has start and end positions and no delimiter column; the CHM file from the same mainframe is fixed-width; a pipe-delimited file would not need positions.
- Confirm with: mainframe team. A sample file (Q16) settles this at once.
- Risk: the wrong format parses zero rows on the first run. Switching the flat-file connection is cheap if known early.

**Q3. What is the file name, and does it carry a date and time?** Reference: In Scope "Create a new LNET import job"; the CHM rule that reads the report date from the file name.

- Recommendation: ask the mainframe team to follow the CHM convention, for example TPTIPRIOR.D{MMDD}T{HH}. If the name has no date token, use the file's modified date as the report date.
- Why: the existing date-parsing script can be reused, and for a one-time load the report date only feeds the summary row.
- Confirm with: mainframe team.
- Risk: low.

**Q4. Where is the file dropped, and who sets up the folder?** Reference: In Scope "import rules similar to CHM" (the FPA folder lookup).

- Recommendation: a new FPA destination row for job name LAS\_Import\_Outsourcing\_HMPriorData pointing at a separate folder, for example inbound\\CHMPrior, in each environment, seeded by a deployment script in db\_efidelivery.
- Why: the CHM package processes every file in its folder, so the TPTI file must not land in inbound\\CHM. Using the same FPA mechanism keeps the operations procedure identical.
- Confirm with: infrastructure or DBA for the folder on the file server per environment; Tech Lead for the names.
- Risk: a shared folder would let the CHM job pick up the TPTI file and fail.

**Q9. Does the file have a header or trailer row with record counts?** Reference: In Scope "reconciliation of imported records"; the CHM count check compares mainframe counts with the SQL count.

- Recommendation: assume no header or trailer. Reconcile the SSIS row count against loaded plus skipped plus rejected rows and report all four numbers. If a trailer exists, parse it and add the mainframe total to the comparison.
- Why: the layout shows no control record; counting rows ourselves works either way.
- Confirm with: mainframe team.
- Risk: an unexpected trailer row would be rejected as one bad data row and logged, which is harmless.

**Q10. The file has no lender, customer or loan. How is the file summary recorded?** Reference: Synopsis "all customers' datasets"; the CHM summary row is written per lender.

- Recommendation: one summary row per file with a placeholder lender value (ALL), written by a small variant of the summary SP that does not loop by lender.
- Why: usp\_CHM\_ImportFileSummary\_IU requires a lender and stamps staging rows per lender, which has no meaning for this file.
- Confirm with: Tech Lead.
- Risk: none.

**Q11. How is a one-time job set up, and what happens to it afterwards?** Reference: story title "One time".

- Recommendation: create the job enabled but without a schedule; a DBA or developer starts it once per environment after the file arrives; keep the package and job so a re-run is possible; the Product Owner decides whether to disable the job after the Production load.
- Why: a manual start avoids an accidental second load and matches how Production deployments are run.
- Confirm with: Product Owner and Tech Lead.
- Risk: low.

**Q13. Is any SSRS report expected?** Reference: Synopsis "most likely follow the same ... SSRS reporting structure currently used by the CHM import process"; US-138027 In Scope "accessible for reporting, auditing, and downstream processing".

- Recommendation: no new or changed SSRS report in these three stories. The new table is queryable for reporting, and HandleManualCHMReport stays as it is.
- Why: no acceptance criterion names a report. The HM report shows completed items with the tax information processors entered, not mainframe history.
- Confirm with: Product Owner.
- Risk: if the business expects the history on the HM report, that is a new story.

**Q14. When one row fails, stop everything or log it and continue? Where are errors logged?** Reference: AC2 "Import failures are logged with clear error messages".

- Recommendation: row-level failures are written to outs\_CHM\_ImportErrors through usp\_CHM\_LogImportError with file name, row number, the raw record, a category (PARSE, RULE, DUPLICATE, LINK) and a plain-language message, and the load continues. File-level failures (missing folder, unreadable file, SQL error) stop the package, set the FPA row to Failed and send the e-mail, exactly as CHM does. A completion e-mail reports loaded, skipped and failed counts.
- Why: this is a one-time historical load, so loading most rows and reporting the rest is more useful than stopping at the first bad row. The CHM batch stops on the first row error and leaves the batch half done, which is a known weakness. The error table was built for this purpose and is unused today.
- Confirm with: Tech Lead and Product Owner.
- Risk: none.

**Q15. How are amounts and dates encoded?** Reference: AC1 layout comments "Amount 9v2 (123456789v12)", "CCYYMMDD".

- Recommendation: treat numeric fields as unsigned with two implied decimals (00000012345 means 123.45); dates as CCYYMMDD with zeros or blanks meaning no date. Keep the raw text in the raw columns; fill the typed columns with TRY\_CONVERT and log a RULE error when a value does not convert, while still storing the row.
- Why: US-138027 AC3 requires the received values to stay untouched; typed copies make the data usable without re-parsing.
- Confirm with: mainframe team, including whether penalties can be negative or signed.
- Risk: low.

**Q16. Can we get a sample file now?** Reference: AC1.

- Recommendation: request a sample from the mainframe team immediately, even a few records. Until it arrives, build and test with a synthetic file generated from the layout, but do not sign off QA without a real file.
- Why: Dev has no CHM data and no TPTI file; parsing the real file is the biggest technical risk in the story.
- Confirm with: mainframe team.
- Risk: this blocks completion of the story.

**N6. If the same file is loaded twice, what should happen?** Reference: US-138027 AC3 (never overwrite) and AC9 (no duplicates), applied to an import re-run.

- Recommendation: skip rows already present by natural key (Agency ID, Tax ID, Tax Year, Add Timestamp, Update Timestamp), count them as skipped and never overwrite.
- Why: keeps AC3 true and makes a re-run after a partial failure safe.
- Confirm with: Product Owner for the key.
- Risk: a corrected mainframe record with the same key would be skipped, which is acceptable for a one-time dump.

## Part 3b - Open questions and recommendations: US-138027 (store and linkage)

Seven questions are open for the linkage story. N2 is the one business decision that changes the data model, so it should be answered first.

**Q7. Is the file's Tax ID the LERETA tax ID or the customer's tax ID, and is it formatted?** Reference: In Scope link key "Agency ID, Tax ID"; AC6 and AC7.

- Recommendation: match on outs\_OpenItems.OIR\_TAX\_ID (the LERETA tax ID), comparing a normalized form on both sides (upper case, dashes and spaces removed), while storing the raw value untouched.
- Why: the TPTI record is a mainframe parcel record keyed by agency and the mainframe's own tax ID, which LERETANet holds in OIR\_TAX\_ID. The customer's ID is in OIR\_ORIG\_TAX\_ID and differs per lender. Dev rows show OIR\_TAX\_ID values with embedded spaces, so a raw equality match would miss parcels.
- Confirm with: BA and mainframe team.
- Risk: the wrong column means no links at all. A test of the sample file against Dev HTC rows will show the match rate before go-live.

**N1. Which rows in outs\_OpenItems count as a CHM record that can own prior data?** Reference: Synopsis "link the corresponding CHM record"; AC6 and AC7.

- Recommendation: rows with OIR\_REC\_TYPE = HTC and isHMItem = 1, which is what the CHM import creates when the handle-manual indicator is set.
- Why: this is the same definition the HM report uses, and it excludes the 3.36 million HTC rows in Dev that are not handle-manual items.
- Confirm with: Product Owner.
- Risk: widening to all HTC rows would multiply the parent candidates and the collisions in N2.

**N2. When several Open CHM items (different loans or lenders) are on the same parcel, which one gets the history?** Reference: In Scope "one-to-many relationship" and "link priors only once for a given tax ID/agency ID"; AC8.

- Recommendation: keep the strict one-to-many model with one ParentOIR\_ID per prior row. In the sweep, the most recently imported Open item on the parcel claims the rows; in the CHM import, the first Open item to arrive claims them. Write an informational error-log row for the items that did not receive links.
- Why: the story explicitly asks for one parent and a once-only link. A link table (many-to-many) would contradict that wording and make the UI read ambiguous. Collisions will happen: Dev has 61,294 HM items on 44,409 distinct parcel-year keys.
- Confirm with: Product Owner. This is a business decision: should every loan on the parcel see the history? If yes, the model becomes a link table and "only once" has to be redefined.
- Risk: deciding this after the table is built means schema rework.

**N3. What counts as Open for AC7?** Reference: AC7 "existing CHM record status is Open".

- Recommendation: isProcessed = 0 and no COP user-tracking row with isComplete = 1. That includes items shown as New, Open, Worked, Follow-up and Research, and excludes Completed, Pending QC and Completed-No QC.
- Why: all of those sub-states are still being worked; the COP screens derive their Status column from the same flags.
- Confirm with: Product Owner and Tech Lead.
- Risk: low.

**N4. Can a link ever be moved or added again later?** Reference: AC9; In Scope "link priors only once".

- Recommendation: never moved automatically. Re-importing a linked item changes nothing. A new Open item on a parcel whose prior rows are already linked to a completed item receives nothing.
- Why: simplest rule, fully auditable, and literally what "only once" says.
- Confirm with: Product Owner. Processors of a later loan on the same parcel will not see the history unless N2 changes the model.
- Risk: a business expectation gap if later loans are expected to see history.

**N5. When exactly does linking run?** Reference: AC6 (on CHM import) and AC7 (existing Open records); US-138028 In Scope "Update the CHM import process".

- Recommendation: both. A sweep runs as the last step of the prior-data package; a single-record call runs inside the CHM import for every new or existing Open record. Both use the same stored procedure.
- Why: the sweep clears the backlog on day one; the per-record call handles everything that arrives afterwards; one SP means the rules cannot drift.
- Confirm with: Tech Lead.
- Risk: the per-record call adds one indexed lookup per CHM row, which is negligible.

**N11. Are the proposed table name and shape acceptable?** Reference: AC1 "A new SQL table is created".

- Recommendation: outs\_CHM\_PriorData and outs\_CHM\_PriorData\_Staging in LASOutsourcing, with raw, typed, load-audit and linkage columns and the two indexes listed in 2.3.
- Why: follows the outs\_CHM\_\* naming of the existing CHM objects; raw columns satisfy AC3, typed columns satisfy the reporting need, the link column satisfies AC5 to AC8.
- Confirm with: Tech Lead and DBA.
- Risk: none.

## Part 3c - Open questions and recommendations: US-138028 (UI and CHM import linkage)

Six questions are open for the UI story. The approved UI design (N7) is the blocking one; N8 decides how much of the existing page changes.

**N7. Which fields and layout does the approved UI design specify?** Reference: AC6 "displays all configured prior data fields according to the approved UI design".

- Recommendation: attach the design to the story. Until then, the default is one read-only block per tax year inside the Prior Information panel, labelled as mainframe history, showing Tax Year, Bill Type, Pay Code, Install and Penalty for installments 1 to 4, Penalty Interest, Sales Number, Redeem Amt 1 with Good Until 1, Redeem Amt 2 with Good Until 2, Handle Manual indicator, last updated (user and date) and added (user and date).
- Why: these are the labels processors already use in the panel, and the list covers every attribute the file carries.
- Confirm with: Product Owner and whoever approved the design.
- Risk: rework of the markup if the design differs from the default.

**N8. Inside the existing Prior Information panel, how do the mainframe rows behave?** Reference: AC1, AC2 and AC5 of this story; US-138027 AC3 and AC4 (prior data is never modified or merged).

- Recommendation: a read-only block at the top of the panel. The Save button ignores it; the user-entered rows and their save path stay as they are; both are shown when both exist; the No. of Years and Add Years controls are not touched; nothing is copied automatically. A later story could add a "copy to prior info" action if processors want it.
- Why: US-138027 forbids modifying or merging the prior data, and keeping the editable rows and their JavaScript untouched is the smallest change to a live screen.
- Confirm with: Product Owner.
- Risk: if the business wants the mainframe rows pre-filled as editable rows, the save path and row identity handling must change, which is a larger and riskier change.

**N9. Should the history also show on completed items and the read-only history views?** Reference: AC1 "The Loan Details page includes a Prior Data section for CHM loans".

- Recommendation: yes, wherever OpenItemLoanDetails.aspx is rendered, including completed and history views, because the binding happens in one place and the block is read-only.
- Why: consistent behaviour for the same page and no extra effort.
- Confirm with: Product Owner.
- Risk: none.

**N10. Is the story number US-138028?** Reference: the pasted title reads "US-13028".

- Recommendation: use US-138028 for folders, scripts and PR titles.
- Confirm with: Product Owner.
- Risk: naming only.

**N12. Where in the CHM import does the linkage call go?** Reference: In Scope "Update the CHM import process to identify and link matching prior data records".

- Recommendation: in usp\_OI\_CHM\_ImportProcess\_OIRecord, right after the new-or-existing branch where the OIR\_ID is known in both paths, guarded by the N1 and N3 rules and wrapped in its own error handler so a linkage failure is logged as a LINK error and never fails the CHM record.
- Why: one place to maintain; the two large insert and update SPs (870 and 1,971 lines) stay untouched; the wrapper already holds the OIR\_ID.
- Confirm with: Tech Lead, because this SP runs in the live CHM import and the team rules require sign-off before a shared SP changes.
- Risk: regression in the CHM import. Mitigation: the error handler, plus the existing CHM test-data script (SQL/Test-Data/US117259\_TestData\_CHM.sql) to seed Dev items for regression testing.

**N13. Which story owns the CHM-import linkage change?** Reference: US-138027 AC6 and AC7 versus US-138028 In Scope line 1, which describe the same change.

- Recommendation: deliver the import-side linkage under US-138027 and the UI under US-138028.
- Why: US-138027 defines the rules; keeping the SQL change with its rules makes the deployment package self-contained.
- Confirm with: Product Owner or Scrum Master.
- Risk: naming and script-folder placement only.

